				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/profaurelius" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7010076); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7010076&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605837925" alt="ProfAurelius" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7010076)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/profaurelius" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7010076); return false;" >ProfAurelius</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605511268 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Nov 2020 07:21</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819752">
									

<p>Well, regarding to Sumerian myth, Enkidu was a beast-man made from clay that was created in response to the tyranny of King Gilgamesh in the Epic of Gilgamesh. In the epic, Enkidu was formed from the breath of the gods, nature at its purest, and made in the image of a long distant warrior/god depending on your interpretation. It's speculative that the image Enkidu was based of was Enki, the Sumerian God of Creation, which he later known as Ea.</p>
<p>For my SCP and the connection with SCP-073, known as Cain, I took the root that the image that was used was that of the long distant warrior: Cain's brother, SCP SCP-076-2 (Able). Hence, the connection it has with SCP-073. As to why it's SCP-073 and not SCP SCP-076-2, it's because I don't think the Foundation would allow a meeting between a volatile entity and an entity that was created by the beings that cursed said volatile entity. Enkidu was an experiment of trying to recreate the perfect warrior that used to be under control of its creator, Able. This was inspired by the tale of Able and his servitude to the Daevas.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Regarding with the connection with SCP-4046(Gaia), it's been said that one of the materials that created Enkidu was nature at it's purest in the form of Clay. This would allow Enkidu to morph nature itself into weapons that would aid him. I decided to take a more creative liberty in that it can change any terrestrial material into the same metal that composed 10% of it's body. With the connection of Gaia, the entities that created Enkidu used the purest form of its manifestation to help create Enkidu. This, in turn, developed a rather unnatural familial connection with the SCP for Enkidu, seeing it as some sort of Mother Figure.</p>
<p>As to what the entity was doing during first contact with the SCP, it was trying to make sense as to what caused its reactivation. This would be the first scene the MTF would witness with numerous metallic spheres floating around it. It was trying to find some sort of information relay that would help explain to it what was going on. But seeing as how Able was the template used to create the entity, at the first sign of being targeted by weapons, the entity lashes out violently and began attacking the MTF forces. Not only is it immune to firearms and explosives, it has the ability to morph the metallic spheres into copies of the weapons that is showed to it. It would also show capabilities of retaining info of weapons that it has learned throughout the ages. But its most dangerous ability is the fact it can perfectly mimic the physiology and abilities of any creature, even some of the SCPs. The only difference you can find is by touch. Despite looking like flesh, it feels like clay at some parts and metal in others. The only known exception is, of course, Cain and Able.</p>
<p>As to why it births the &quot;legend of heroes&quot;, Enkidu is just one of many experiments that were created by the same entities that cursed Able. It was sent as a test run to see how reliable it was as a replacement for Able. This test run would ultimately fail as the entity had befriended the target of its test run and that would eventually lead to its re-possession in the form of the Epic's death version where Enkidu &quot;crumbled like brittle clay and returned back to its creators&quot;. This, of course, meant that Enkidu was returned back to its creators and had the equivalent of having a system shut down. Upon interview, Enkidu would then reveal that many heroes in mythology were, in one shape or form, experiments trying to recreate its creator's perfect servant-warrior.</p>
</div>
</div>
